      "pov": "Leah",
      "start": 0,
      "text": "Leah carried the ledger with both hands, although it was no heavier than the loaf she had bought that morning. Rain had softened the brown paper around it. She turned the parcel so the wet fold faced down and set it on the dry half of Jonas's desk.\n\nThe office window looked onto an empty slip. The chain that usually held the ferry had been looped around a post. Its lowest link tapped the wood when the wind moved. Leah counted the taps until Jonas came back from the stove.\n\n\"You could have left that at the hall,\" he said.\n\n\"They told me to bring it here.\"\n\n\"The hall has excellent directions. Usually to somewhere else.\"\n\nShe took the receipt from her pocket. The ink had run along one edge, but the line naming Jonas remained legible. He flattened it with the heel of his hand. Neither of them touched the parcel.\n\nLeah had not opened the ledger or read any of its pages. She had been told only that it belonged at the ferry office before the evening crossing. She did not know why the clerk had chosen her to carry it. At the hall she had asked whether the ferry was running. The clerk had handed her the receipt again.\n\nJonas moved the brass paperweight from one corner of the desk to the other. Under it lay a square of blotting paper, clean except for a blue crescent. He put the receipt there and returned the weight.\n\n\"Will you sign?\" Leah asked.\n\n\"I can sign that it reached the office.\"\n\n\"That's what it says.\"\n\n\"Then we've found a document with modest ambitions.\"\n\nHis pen made a small notch in the paper before the ink began to flow. Leah watched the notch. She wanted the signed receipt back. Once she had it, she could leave without deciding whether the empty slip was any of her business.\n\nJonas looked at the clock. Its minute hand was at twelve, but the second hand had stopped between marks. He shook his wristwatch and listened to it.\n\n\"That one hasn't worked since Thursday,\" he said, nodding at the clock.\n\nLeah moved her hand toward the receipt. He lifted the paperweight for her. The ledger remained wrapped. Outside, the loose chain struck the post twice, then stopped.\n"
