      "pov": "Jonas",
      "start": 0,
      "text": "After Leah left, Jonas pushed the chair under the desk with his knee. She had not seen him take the copper key from the small drawer. He waited until her steps had passed the office window, then slid the key beneath the brass paperweight. It fitted along the edge of the blotting paper.\n\nHe did not call after her. They had not discussed the key or the drawer, and he sent her no message. The ledger was still wrapped on the desk. The signed receipt had gone with Leah.\n\nAt the stove he held his palms near the iron without touching it. Someone had left a damp glove across the rail. He moved it to the hook and replaced it immediately when water dripped onto his sleeve.\n\nHis reflection in the dark window showed a green streak above his left ear. He had dyed that lock the previous evening after years of wearing it black. The color was his daughter's choice. She had offered to do the whole head; he had negotiated the small strip. He pressed the wet ends flat and found green on his thumb.\n\nA bell sounded from the hall. It was too early for the crossing bell, unless the stopped clock had misled everyone else as well. Jonas opened the office door. No one stood on the step. Across the yard, two men were moving a crate under the shelter. Neither looked toward the slip.\n\nHe could take the parcel back to the hall. That would mean signing a second receipt, explaining the first one and answering the question Leah had already asked. Instead he checked the string around the ledger. The knot held. He left it tied.\n\nThe chain moved once against the post. Jonas watched the empty water until his hands cooled. He was waiting for the ferry, and for the moment when waiting would become a decision someone could name.\n"
